WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.537
TypeScript can help developers build safer applications by making intent clearer.

2
00:00:05.537 --> 00:00:15.596
Types can describe the shape of a request object, the values a function expects, the roles a component understands, or the result a service should return.

3
00:00:15.596 --> 00:00:20.197
That clarity improves code review and catches many mistakes earlier.

4
00:00:20.197 --> 00:00:24.642
But TypeScript does not automatically make an application secure.

5
00:00:24.642 --> 00:00:30.023
The important reason is that TypeScript types are not available as runtime proof.

6
00:00:30.023 --> 00:00:44.528
Data from a browser form, a JSON request body, local storage, a database, a third-party API, a message queue, or an environment variable can still be missing, malformed, outdated, or inappropriate for the current action.

7
00:00:44.528 --> 00:00:48.505
The application must validate and enforce trust at runtime.

8
00:00:48.505 --> 00:00:52.170
TypeScript applications also run in different places.

9
00:00:52.170 --> 00:00:55.757
Browser code handles rendering and user interaction.

10
00:00:55.757 --> 00:01:03.633
Node. js services, serverless functions, and backend APIs handle data access, secrets, sessions, and authorization.

11
00:01:03.633 --> 00:01:07.688
Shared packages can influence many consuming applications.

12
00:01:07.688 --> 00:01:11.977
Each runtime has its own trust boundaries and responsibilities.

13
00:01:11.977 --> 00:01:23.441
A secure TypeScript mindset asks where trust enters the system, where data becomes validated, where authorization is enforced, and where sensitive information is handled.

14
00:01:23.441 --> 00:01:27.106
The goal is not to write the most complex types possible.

15
00:01:27.106 --> 00:01:34.592
The goal is to make risky assumptions visible, reviewable, and testable so safe behavior becomes the normal path.

16
00:01:34.592 --> 00:01:47.225
Teams should also document these decisions so new maintainers can understand which code is trusted, which data is checked, and which framework controls must stay enabled during maintenance.
